{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $B={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $B={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -5 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec1398e5-e972-59d8-bca4-9aeed1536330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461225} {:answer/id #uuid "a135e004-34a6-5207-9ef4-ec52ff84fde2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec1398e5-e972-59d8-bca4-9aeed1536330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461225} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dcf6a9c-0fbc-5e47-87a2-e4afc24a5b1a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :